            "pages": {
              "end": 4,
              "start": 4
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Can you be specific about who those persons would be? You say not a misdemeanant drug user. So who would be caught in the 843(b)?"
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "0a9a0c68880603e24fca1c19c16a4838ba670ca9f19ffdee4a3925eede71c8ae",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-0a9a0c688806-0001-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 20,
              "start": 20
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Justice Ginsburg, jUSTICE GINSBURG: That was changed in 1970, wasn't it? Wasn't the text \"offense\" originally, and then Congress changed it to \"felony\"?"
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "0a9a0c68880603e24fca1c19c16a4838ba670ca9f19ffdee4a3925eede71c8ae",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-0a9a0c688806-0001-0004"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 36,
              "start": 36
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Justice Ginsburg, jUSTICE GINSBURG: And it may be that in certain States voting rights are removed, and there is on this person's record forever that he is a felon."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "0a9a0c68880603e24fca1c19c16a4838ba670ca9f19ffdee4a3925eede71c8ae",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-0a9a0c688806-0002-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 38,
              "start": 38
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Is there another example in the law, anywhere in the law, where -- and there may be, I'm asking -- which you've come across, where we have an illegal business and there is a customer; and all the customer does is be a customer; and is there an example where just because he's a customer in a statutory provision that normally has a lesser penalty -- all right; imagine those circumstances -- you still can punish him as if he -- as if he ran the business?"
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "0a9a0c68880603e24fca1c19c16a4838ba670ca9f19ffdee4a3925eede71c8ae",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-0a9a0c688806-0002-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 41,
              "start": 41
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Justice Ginsburg, jUSTICE GINSBURG: What about the -- the statement that in the manual -- maybe this is incorrect -- but that the charging policy of the Department of Justice instructs prosecutors to charge the most serious offense supported by the facts? And if that's true, then the Assistant U.S. Attorney would have no choice. The most serious offense is not misdemeanor simple possession, but it is the violation of 843(b)."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Alito",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "0a9a0c68880603e24fca1c19c16a4838ba670ca9f19ffdee4a3925eede71c8ae",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-0a9a0c688806-0003-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 57,
              "start": 57
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Justice Alito, jUSTICE ALITO: The buyer/seller rule would apply in your view in exactly the same way."
      }
    ],
